          %h3#uniqueness_of_constructions Uniqueness of Constructions
          %p
            It is well-known that objects defined by
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/universal+property"} universal properties
            in a category, such as
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/limits"} limits
            and
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/colimits"} colimits
            %em skeletal
            category, where any two isomorphic objects are equal, such objects will in fact be unique on the nose. However, under the most appropriate definition of “unique,” this is
            %em not
            true (in general), because of the presence of automorphisms.
          %p
            More explicitly, consider the notion of
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/cartesian+product"} cartesian product
            in a category. Although we colloquially speak of “a product” of objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
