                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/generalized+the"} generalized sense
            that makes sense in any category. A formal way to say this is that the “category of products of
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            and
            = succeed ",\u201D" do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi B
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} B
            %em equivalent
            to the
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+category"} trivial category
            %em isomorphic
            to the trivial category even when the ambient category is skeletal.
          %p
            (It is true in a few cases, though, that skeletality implies uniqueness on the nose. For instance, a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/terminal+object"} terminal object
            can have no nonidentity automorphisms, so in a skeletal category, a terminal object (if one exists) really is unique on the nose.)
          %h2#in_dependent_type_theory In dependent type theory
          %p
            All the discussion above pertains to
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/set-level+foundations"} set-level foundations
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/intensional+type+theory"} intensional type theory
            such as
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/homotopy+type+theory"} homotopy type theory
          %p
            A first guess is to define a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/precategory"} precategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            to be skeletal if for any objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b
            there is a function
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mi a
                    %mo ≅
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                    %mo →
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mi a
                    %mo =
                    %mi b
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vert a\cong b\Vert \to (a=b)
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} b
            are merely isomorphic then they are equal. (Here
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mo −
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vert-\Vert
            denotes the
            = succeed ".)" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncation"} propositional truncation
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/type"} type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ob(C)
            of objects is not a set, then
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b
            is not a
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/proposition"} proposition
          %p
            Arguably a better definition is to say that a precategory
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is
            %strong skeletal
            if for any objects
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ,
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a,b
            the canonical function
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo →
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (a=b) \to \Vert a\cong b\Vert
          %p
            is an equivalence. Since
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi b
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ‖
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \Vert a\cong b\Vert
            is always a proposition, this entails that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo =
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a=b
            is also a proposition. Thus, this implies that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi ob
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi C
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} ob(C)
            %em is
            a set, i.e. that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi C
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} C
            is a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/strict+category"} strict category
          %p
            Note the analogy between this condition and the notion of
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/univalent+category"} univalent category
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi a
                  %mo ≅
                  %mi b
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} a\cong b
            is always a proposition (since it is equivalent to its propositional truncation), and thus the category is
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gaunt+category"} gaunt
          %p
            Unlike in set-level foundations, in intensional type theory not every precategory (and not even every univalent category) has a skeleton. Indeed, if the axiom
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/sets+cover"} sets cover
            fails, then there can be univalent categories that are not weakly equivalent to any strict category. For this reason, the notion of skeleton tends to be less useful outside of set-level foundations, although particular concrete examples may turn out to be skeletal.
          %p
            In
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/simplicial+type+theory"} simplicial type theory
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/precategories"} precategories
            to
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+types"} Segal types
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/synthetic+mathematics"} synthetic
            = succeed "-precategories." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                    %mn ∞
                    %mo ,
                    %mn 1
                    %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} (\infty,1)
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+in+simplicial+type+theory"} isomorphism
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} iso
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{iso}_A(x, y)
            defined from the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/directed+interval+type"} directed interval type
            = succeed "," do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi 𝕀
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathbb{I}
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Segal+type"} Segal type
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
            is
            %strong skeletal
            if for all
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x:A
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                  %mo :
                  %mi A
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y:A
            the canonical function which by the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/J+rule"} J rule
            takes an identification of elements
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi p
                  %mo :
                  %mi x
                  %msub
                    %mo =
                    %mi A
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} p:x =_A y
            to a witness that
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi x
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} x
            and
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi y
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} y
            are
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+in+a+Segal+type"} merely isomorphic
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi t
                  %mo .
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo :
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} iso
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \vert J(\lambda t.\mathrm{id}_A(t), x, y, p) \vert:[\mathrm{iso}_A(x, y)]
          %p
            is an
            = succeed "," do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+types"} equivalence of types
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} [
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} ]
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} [A]
            is the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/propositional+truncation"} propositional truncation
            of
            = succeed "." do
              %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                %semantics
                  %mrow
                    %mi A
                  %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} A
          .maruku-equation
            %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "block", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
              %semantics
                %mrow
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isSkeletal
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ≔
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi x
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %munder
                    %mo{:lspace => "thinmathspace", :rspace => "thinmathspace"} ∏
                    %mrow
                      %mi y
                      %mo :
                      %mi A
                  %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} isEquiv
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi p
                  %mo .
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mi J
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi λ
                  %mi t
                  %mo .
                  %msub
                    %mi{:mathvariant => "normal"} id
                    %mi A
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} (
                  %mi t
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo ,
                  %mi x
                  %mo ,
                  %mi y
                  %mo ,
                  %mi p
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} |
                  %mo{:stretchy => "false"} )
                %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} \mathrm{isSkeletal}(A) \coloneqq \prod_{x:A} \prod_{y:A} \mathrm{isEquiv}(\lambda p.\vert J(\lambda t.\mathrm{id}_A(t), x, y, p) \vert)
          %p
            One gets the usual notion of a
            %em skeletal category
            if all
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/hom+types"} hom types
            of a Segal type are
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/h-sets"} h-sets
          %h2#examples Examples
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                Every
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/poset"} poset
                is skeletal.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/walking+parallel+pair"} walking parallel pair
                is a skeletal category which is not a poset.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/free+object"} free
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                on a
                %span.newWikiWord
                  directed acyclic graph
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/directed+acyclic+graph"} ?
                is skeletal.
            %li
              %p
                The
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/category"} category
                of
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Archimedean+ordered+fields"} Archimedean ordered fields
                and
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/monotonic"} monotonic
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/field+homomorphisms"} field homomorphisms
                is skeletal.
          %p
            All of the above examples are in fact
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gaunt+categories"} gaunt
          %ul
            %li
              The
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/delooping"} delooping
              of a
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/group"} group
              is a skeletal category that is not gaunt (unless the group is
              = succeed ")." do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/trivial+group"} trivial
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rezk+completion"} Rezk completion
              is no longer skeletal according to the above improved definition.
          %h3#PosetsAsSkeletaOfProsets Posets as skeleta of prosets
          %p
            In terms of
            = succeed "-theory" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%28n%2Cr%29-category"} (n,r)-category
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preordered+sets"} preordered sets
            with
            %em
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/thin+categories"} thin categories
            or
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/%280%2C1%29-categories"} (0,1)-categories
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/equivalence+of+categories"} equivalent
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/partial+order"} partial order
            inside a
            = succeed "." do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/preorder"} preorder
          %p
            For more details (and more precise statements, see at
            = succeed "." do
              %em
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/relation+between+preorders+and+%280%2C1%29-categories"} relation between preorders and (0,1)-categories
          %h2#related_concepts Related concepts
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal+groupoid"} skeletal groupoid
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/gaunt+category"} gaunt category
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Rezk+complete+type"} Rezk complete type
            %li
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/isomorphism+in+simplicial+type+theory"} isomorphism in simplicial type theory
          %h2#references References
          %ul
            %li
              %p
                %span.newWikiWord>
                  J. R. Isbell
                  %a{:href => "/nlab/new/J.+R.+Isbell"} ?
                , and F. B. Wright.
                = succeed "." do
                  %em Another equivalent form of the axiom of choice
            %li#MacLane71
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Saunders+MacLane"}> Saunders MacLane
                , p. 91 of:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Categories+for+the+Working+Mathematician"} Categories for the Working Mathematician
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://link.springer.com/book/10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8"} doi:10.1007/978-1-4757-4721-8
            %li#RiehlCTInContext
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Emily+Riehl"}> Emily Riehl
                , p. 34 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em
                    %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Category+Theory+in+Context"} Category Theory in Context
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://emilyriehl.github.io/files/context.pdf"} pdf
            %li#Richter19
              %p
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Birgit+Richter"}> Birgit Richter
                , §2.6 in:
                = succeed "," do
                  %em From categories to homotopy theory
                = succeed "," do
                  %strong 188
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://doi.org/10.1017/9781108855891"} doi:10.1017/9781108855891
                = succeed "," do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/richter/catbook.html"} book webpage
                = succeed "&rbrack;" do
                  %a{:href => "https://www.math.uni-hamburg.de/home/richter/bookdraft.pdf"} pdf
          %p See also:
          %ul
            %li
              Wikipedia,
              %em
                %a{:href => "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Skeleton_(category_theory)"} Skeleton_(category_theory)
          %p
            Presenting a
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/pretorsion+theory"} pretorsion theory
            on
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Cat"} Cat
            whose torsion(-free) objects are the
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/groupoids"} groupoids
            (
            %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal+categories"}> skeletal categories
            , respectively), hence whose “trivial objects” are the
            = succeed ":" do
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/skeletal+groupoids"} skeletal groupoids
          %ul
            %li
              %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Francis+Borceux"}> Francis Borceux
              ,
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Federico+Campanini"} Federico Campanini
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Marino+Gran"} Marino Gran
              = succeed "," do
                %a.existingWikiWord{:href => "/nlab/show/Walter+Tholen"} Walter Tholen
              %em
                Groupoids and skeletal categories form a pretorsion theory in
                %math.maruku-mathml{:display => "inline", :xmlns => "http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML"}
                  %semantics
                    %mrow
                      %mi Cat
                    %annotation{:encoding => "application/x-tex"} Cat
              \&amp;lbrack;
              %a{:href => "https://arxiv.org/abs/2207.08487"}> arXiv:2207.08487
              \&amp;rbrack;
        .revisedby
          %p
            Last revised on July 13, 2025 at 15:40:01.
